      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
Your house has a few locations where significant air leakage exists:

** The ceiling above the furnace is not well sealed at all and a significant amount of air transfers up into the attic from around both the chimney and the metal flashing against the ceiling. I suspect this heat loss into the attic is the reason why this house has so much ice damming on the right side of the roof.

** There is a 4" outside fresh air supply pipe located in the upper wall behind the furnace. I usually see these directly connected to the top left corner of these Intertherm furnaces (where the knockout is) and the pipe will usually have an inline damper that you can manually adjust depending upon how much fresh air the house needs. If the house has very low humidity, then adjust the damper to be more closed. If the house has too much humidity or the air inside feels stale &amp; stuffy, then adjust the damper to be more open.

** There is an open hole below the kitchen sink (and adjoining bathtub on the other side of the wall) that appears to be open into the crawlspace below. I sense the hole is quite large as I could see (with my Infrared Camera) alot of cold air creeping up into the wall between the kitchen cabinets &amp; the laundry area behind. You will need to ensure the ceiling of the crawlspace is fully air sealed and then keep lots of insulation below the water &amp; drain pipes that go into the floor.

** The room air conditioner installed in the front window allows alot of air leakage &amp; these should be completely removed from windows during the winter heating season.

** The front door does not appear to seal well and may need new weather-stripping or perhaps install a new ENERGY STAR® qualified door.

** The rear bedroom windows are "sliders" that (by design) allow more air leakage than casement windows. There are in pretty decent shape, so I'm not sold that replacing them is a cost-effective measure ......... unless you choose to complete a full exterior wall insulation/siding upgrade (which I do not believe is on your radar at this time).

Savings:
The air leakage (heat loss) from this house is significant and I expect a minimum 50% reduction in air leakage if you address the locations identified above. This is estimated to save $ 829 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 19.1 GJ.

There are currently no rebates for air sealing unless you are participating in our Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), which is scheduled to come to a close on January 31, 2024. I did model this opportunity on one of your other homes (#18 Ptarmigan Avenue) as an FYI, however this house is 1/2 of a duplex, where you would need to upgrade the wall insulation &amp; siding (perhaps windows also) on both sides within the next 11 months. I think the air leakage savings outlined above are likely the best payback that is immediate &amp; ongoing every year .......... without trying to perform extra work just to obtain a DHERP rebate.

If you think you will complete a full exterior house retrofit (adding a minimum R10 insulation &amp; new siding) during 2023, please let us know.
</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <windows />
                <doors>
Your house has 2 exterior entrance doors and the one in the front has some air leakage around it &amp; along the bottom door sweep. You may be able to have new weather-stripping installed this summer, or perhaps you may choose to replace it with a new ENERGY STAR® qualified unit. If you replace them, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.

Savings:
By replacing this front door system with one that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 10 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 0.2 GJ. There are currently no eligible rebates available for upgrading exterior doors in this rental house.</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables>
Renewable Energy Systems

The Arctic Energy Alliance administers the Alternative Energy Technologies Program (AETP) that includes rebates for Solar PV systems, including integrated battery energy storage for NWT residents who are not connected to the hydro-electric grid. 

Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the rear facing roof (South), directly connected to the Norman Wells grid (ie: not including a battery storage system). This is estimated to produce about 3417 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,127, and qualifying for up to $20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program.

For more information and pre-approval rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
https://aea.nt.ca/program/renewable-energy/

NOTE: The GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Please note that only the principal residence of the homeowner is eligible to participate in the Canada Greener Homes Grant program. As this property is a rental unit, it will not be eligible.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA.

PLEASE NOTE: The DHERP is scheduled to end on January 31, 2024, so you would only have this year to complete the required upgrades &amp; have us back for a post-retrofit energy evaluation. Please let us know ASAP if you plan to do this, so we can pre-approve you for funding.

For more information, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Norman Wells, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the bathroom fan &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.6 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current oil furnace is direct vented (as it has a concentric chimney vent system), and is not susceptible to having combustion products back-drafted into the house.

During my assessment of your home, I did see a combination smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following ventilation:
        - Kitchen range hood exhaust fan (does not vent to the outside)
        - Bathroom exhaust fan (vents to the outside but appears older &amp; allows some leakage)
        - Clothes dryer - vented to the outside

If you choose to replace or install or replace any exhaust fans, please select ENERGY STAR® qualified units. For a new bathroom fan, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Norman Wells

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Norman Wells:
Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.54 per kWh thereafter
Oil  - $2.17 per litre 
Wood - $100 per cord self-harvested, $800 per cord purchased
Pellets - $10.00 per bag, $660.00 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Ken Baigent, Contracted Senior Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
Phone: (867) 920-3333
Fax: (867) 873-0303
E-mail: k.baigent@aea.nt.ca
Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
